  "strategy": [
    "Each Computer Engineering paper has four 5-mark questions in Q1, then five 10-mark questions with a choice of attempts. Own-branch papers match, except May 2025, which lists four extra 10-mark parts under Q6.",
    "A Flajolet-Martin numerical has been asked in all 7 CE papers, and Girvan-Newman communities in 6 of 7. Practise both until they are mechanical; they are the safest 10-markers.",
    "Every own-branch paper asks for the MapReduce execution pipeline or its components (4 of 4 papers). DGIM has also been asked in 3 of the 4, so both are strong picks.",
    "CE papers carry R programming questions (17 in all, up to 3 per paper) that sit outside the AI and ML syllabus. Skip them unless you have studied R; other choices cover Q2 to Q6.",
    "Recommendation systems appears in all 11 papers; collaborative filtering and content-based filtering together account for 9 questions, so learn both with one worked example each."
  ]
